 classes for a native image might not work. Direct reflection isn't recommended unless it's required by a public API.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nI wonder if I could use Unsafe to change the return value of Runtime.maxMemory, but that’s complicated. Maybe I could find a JVM flag or system property that allows Runtime.maxMemory to return Long.MAX_VALUE.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"summary_text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"**Considering dynamic access logging**\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nI think I need to execute line 692 in the JVM tests to mark the covered call site, and record dynamic access during the native compile. I'm wondering if the dynamic access compile sees the call site statically when building the native image. Actually, the TrackDynamicAccess might log calls encountered during analysis. \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nHowever, it seems the previous simple direct call didn’t cover line 692, so dynamic access logging was absent. I might need the actual native image execution since native tests include that. I'll need to ensure the dynamic access is logged during compilation and that corresponding lines are covered in JVM tests.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"summary_text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"**Analyzing coverage reports**\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nIt seems my last direct call might have covered the line in JaCoCo, but the dynamic access log was missing because the runtime maxMemory branch wasn't taken during the native image compile. The dynamic coverage report showed that the resource site at line 397 was covered because the JVM test forced a fallback. I’m thinking that the native image compile could have recorded the resource access. \\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nStill, I’m uncertain about line 692, as the JVM test may not have executed it. The direct call for available memory seems to show a finite path, not hitting line 692.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"summary_text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"**Clarifying JVM testing approach**\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nI'm noting that I've hit a line in my JVM test, which suggests that dynamic access logs for invoking are probably already there. The report indicates that the call site is logged as total/uncovered, which means it tracked through dynamic-access logs. Great, so we just need line coverage! I’m exploring the idea of using a child JVM with JaCoCo coverage appended to the parent, but it needs further consideration. It might be simpler to adjust the approach regarding agent generation.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"summary_text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"**Exploring JVM test coverage**\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nI'm considering that the current test line coverage might only come from the parent. Since dynamic access is already logged, I definitely need line coverage. The idea of using a child JVM with the same JaCoCo agent to add line coverage is reasonable, but I need to create a runtime-max-memory agent without ASM. Could I use pre-generated base64 bytes for the agent class? ByteBuddy seems possible, but it's tricky with dependencies. I wonder if adding a test dependency for one library in the `build.gradle` would be acceptable.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"summary_text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"**Considering native test execution**\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nI realized that all tests run under nativeTest too, which complicates things a bit. Our current child process relies on external javac/java tools available on the host. However, a native-image built test binary might still be able to spawn these external tools, which could be okay. I'm thinking about adding a ByteBuddy dependency and dynamically creating an agent jar. But that might make things complicated. Perhaps I can use the ByteBuddy library within the current JVM to create the agent, but we'll definitely need the agent jar for the child JVM.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"summary_text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"**Exploring JVM Agent Options**\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nI’m wondering if an agent could utilize the `java.lang.invoke` and instrumentation APIs to intercept `Runtime.maxMemory`, perhaps through a method handle. I could leverage the existing ASM dependency in the project by searching for the ASM jar in the Gradle cache. However, I ran into issues compiling the generated agent against ASM. It might be easier to create source that imports `org.objectweb.asm.*`, but the build file lacks the ASM dependency. So, I'll see if anything from `org.objectweb.asm` is present in the classpath.\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"summary_text\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"**Assessing ASM Import Options**\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nI’m considering importing internal classes based on a version-specific package that has `internal_<hash>.asm`, but that doesn’t seem stable. Maybe I could use the ASM bundled with the JDK through the `jdk.internal.org.objectweb.asm` package, but it might require specific compile options like `--add-exports java.base/jdk.internal.org.objectweb.asm=ALL-UNNAMED --add-modules java.base`. Since `java.base` is always present, I should verify the setup outside using bash to see if the compiler can access that package.\\\\\\\"}]}\\\"},{\\\"type\\\":\\\"toolCall\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"call_PwN46WEaNrx8Le5fToiACnGt|fc_0229233e24d936e80169eba1c8a92081a396886e116bc2ca93\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"bash\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":{\\\"command\\\":\\\"tmp=$(mktemp -d); cat > $tmp/T.java <<'EOF'\\\\nimport jdk.internal.org.objectweb.asm.ClassReader;\\\\npublic class T { ClassReader r; }\\\\nEOF\\\\njavac --add-exports java.base/jdk.internal.org.objectweb.asm=ALL-UNNAMED $tmp/T.java; echo rc:$?\\\",\\\"timeout\\\":20},\\\"partialJson\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"tmp=$(mktemp -d); cat > $tmp/T.java <<'EOF'\\\\\\\\nimport jdk.internal.org.objectweb.asm.ClassReader;\\\\\\\\npublic class T { ClassReader r; }\\\\\\\\nEOF\\\\\\\\njavac --add-exports java.base/jdk.internal.org.objectweb.asm=ALL-UNNAMED $tmp/T.java; echo rc:$?\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"time